{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Summary\n\nThe completion-verifier close gate cannot produce a verdict for a multi-criteria epic:\nthe PRIMARY agentic run — despite carrying the `record_criterion_verdict` tool, the\ncriterion-id manifest, and ~175KB of prefetched evidence (all child file_impact bodies) —\nissues a stream of reasonable `search_files` queries but NEVER calls\n`record_criterion_verdict`, cycling its query list until budget exhausts. Banking zero, the\nsuccessor-recovery also banks zero, and the deterministic zero-progress guard\n(`src/rebar/llm/workflow/completion_recovery.py:555`) fail-closes with\n`CompletionRecoveryError` (\"completion recovery banked no verdicts before exhausting its\nbudget\"). The close is blocked; only `--force` clears it.\n\nFirst observed closing epic cf3c-1444-863e-49d7 (4 ACs, graph=True, all 4 children\nclosed+certified).\n\n## Proven root cause (runtime-confirmed)\n\nInstrumented `CriterionBank.upsert` + per-run tool histograms across 3 live verifier runs on\ncf3c:\n\n- 1x budget (req_limit=24 / tc_limit=48): primary = 22 search_files + 12 read_file + 2\n  list_directory; successor = 18 search_files + ...; **bank upserts = 0**.\n- 4x budget (req_limit=100 / tc_limit=200): primary = **97** search_files + 12 read_file + 3\n  list_directory; **bank upserts = 0**.\n\nDiscriminating result: search_files calls scale with budget (22 -> 97) while banks stay 0.\n=> NOT a budget shortfall. The model treats banking as optional and defers to a final\nstructured emit it never reaches; it also cycles the SAME ~30 queries ~3x (a\ntermination-awareness failure the runaway-loop breaker misses because the repeats are\ninterspersed, so max_consecutive_repeat=1 / distinct_ratio_window=1.0).\n\nWiring verified correct (not the cause): the primary IS constructed with\n`extra_tools=[record_tool]` and `extra_context=<manifest>`\n(`completion_recovery.py:CompletionAgentStep.run`); `RunnerAgentStep.run` DOES forward both\ninto the RunRequest; `assemble_prefetch(graph=True)` DOES aggregate the subtree file_impact\n(all S3 test/impl/doc bodies) and `fit_within_ceiling` keeps all 119KB (model ceiling 2M).\nSo the model has the tool, the ids, and the evidence in-context — and still never banks.\n\nThe captured queries are reasonable (`require_s3_helper`, `heal_multi_bundle`, exact paths),\nso this is not a query-formulation bug; it is a banking-adherence + termination-awareness\nfailure.\n\n## Legitimacy: defect\n\nViolates a cited design invariant. `completion_verifier.md` §\"Incremental banking\" (lines\n~256-278) and the `completion_recovery.py` module docstring both intend the model to \"bank\neach criterion's verdict incrementally as it works ... if budget runs out mid-verification,\nonly the criteria you have ALREADY banked survive into recovery.\" Banking zero across a full\n(and 4x) budget directly violates that intent.\n\n## Taxonomy (LLM-surface)\n\nPrimarily #16 Instruction-locality (the banking directive lives in the prompt preamble,\nstructurally distant from the per-criterion search/read loop) compounded by #6\nTermination-awareness (re-runs the same tool cycle, cannot recognize \"enough to bank\").\n\n## Proposed resolution (KERNEL, affirmative-framed — to confirm via prompt-perturbation eval)\n\n1. Co-locate an affirmative per-criterion banking directive INTO the manifest that is already\n   appended per-run (`primary_criteria_manifest` / the extra_context seam), e.g.: \"For EACH id\n   below, gather evidence with at most N searches, then IMMEDIATELY call\n   record_criterion_verdict(id, met, evidence) before moving to the next id.\" This addresses\n   #16 (make the rule reachable at the loop point) and #6 (bound per-criterion search + force a\n   bank).\n2. (Optional, structural) For an epic whose every DIRECT child is closed AND certified and\n   whose file_impact_scope is `none` (child-delivered ACs), allow the child-closure evidence to\n   deterministically resolve child-delivered criteria without agentic re-verification —\n   extending the existing 6ec8 child-closure shortcut beyond \"every child is closed\" phrasing.\n\nFix must land under an LLM behavioral eval (pinned settings, multiple samples vs a predeclared\nthreshold, negative control) per the rebar-debug LLM-surface discipline; the RED artifact is\nthat eval failing on the current prompt and passing after the directive change.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A behavioral eval reproduces zero-banking on a >=3-criterion graph=True epic with the\n      current prompt (RED), and passes (>=1 criterion banked, verdict produced) after the fix,\n      at a predeclared threshold over multiple samples.\n- [ ] The completion close gate produces a PASS/FAIL verdict (no CompletionRecoveryError) for a\n      multi-criteria epic whose children are closed+certified, without --force.\n- [ ] No regression on single-criterion task/bug close verdicts (existing gate tests green).\n\n## Phase 2 repair plan (corrected after 9895 recurrence)\n\n### Mechanism and minimal repair\n\nThe confirmed defect is not missing capacity or a hidden record tool. The concrete banking\ncommand currently rides inside ticket_context, which the system prompt explicitly classifies as\nuntrusted data, while the authoritative prompt leaves the commit point at subjective\nconfidence/sufficient-evidence. Replace that split contract with one affirmative finite loop at\nsystem authority:\n\n1. Process manifest ids in order, one current unbanked criterion at a time.\n2. Use prefetched evidence first and at most three additional repository evidence-tool calls for\n   the current id.\n3. Call record_criterion_verdict for that id after the bound; record met=false with the bounded\n   searches when the evidence does not demonstrate the criterion.\n4. Advance only after the tool confirms the bank write. A later discovery may revise an already\n   banked provisional verdict through the existing idempotent overwrite.\n5. Emit the existing authoritative full structured verdict after every criterion is processed.\n\nThis is fail-closed on insufficient evidence, preserves judgment quality through provisional\nrevision, and fits the existing default budget of four model-request steps per criterion (three\nevidence steps plus the record transition). It does not change criteria classification, evidence\nstandards, the completion schema, finalization, signatures, or any close policy.\n\nKeep the criterion manifest inside ticket_context as data only: a heading plus exact ids and\ncriterion excerpts, with no imperative text that conflicts with the prompt's untrusted-input\nboundary. Make the successor system addendum continue the same finite loop over remainder ids;\nremove its subjective sufficient-evidence trigger rather than maintaining a second behavioral\ncontract.\n\n### RED oracle and behavioral eval\n\nFreeze this test contract before code changes:\n\n- authoritative_contract: the Incremental banking section and completion_recovery module contract\n  require criterion progress to survive budget exhaustion.\n- trigger_preconditions: a pinned standard verifier model at temperature 0, at least three dense\n  codebase-verifiable criteria, the real record tool, real file/search tools, and a bounded request\n  budget.\n- production_path: completion.verify_completion through CompletionAgentStep primary and successor\n  recovery, against a disposable git/rebar fixture or a read-only supplied ticket.\n- test_tier: repeated live behavioral eval; a unit test cannot establish model compliance.\n- observable_postcondition: at least one bank write occurs before exhaustion and the gate returns a\n  PASS/FAIL verdict instead of CompletionRecoveryError.\n- negative_control: the existing simple three-criterion fixture continues to bank all criteria and\n  produce the same semantic verdict.\n- pinned settings and threshold: bedrock:us.anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-6, temperature 0, three trials\n  per arm; the frozen current-prompt arm banks in at most one of three trials, while the repaired\n  arm banks at least one criterion and produces a verdict in at least two of three. Report all\n  counts and tool/request consumption.\n\nFirst add the eval in its final form and observe RED against current main. Keep it outside the\nfix worker's view. After the prompt repair, rerun it GREEN, seed the exact old authority/subjective\ncontract as a mutation, and confirm the eval returns RED. Also prove a behavior-preserving prompt\nmetadata/refactor change does not affect the oracle.\n\n### Deterministic regression coverage\n\n- Guard that primary_criteria_manifest emits ids/criterion excerpts as data but no imperative\n  banking command.\n- Guard that the authoritative completion system prompt and successor prompt carry the same finite\n  transition and fail-closed met=false rule.\n- Retain the existing tool signature, bank overwrite, full-coverage finalizer, zero-progress, and\n  single-criterion completion suites unchanged; run their focused suite plus lint/typecheck.\n\n### Caller and compatibility scope\n\nBounded caller sweep before repair: primary_criteria_manifest and successor_system_prompt feed only\nCompletionAgentStep and their tests; the completion-verifier prompt is consumed by the close gate,\nverify-completion, and standing eval solver. The post-fix public behavior is unchanged except that a\ndense run produces a normal evidence-backed PASS/FAIL instead of an infrastructure ERROR. Existing\nsingle-criterion callers, completion verdict schema, signatures, CLI/MCP/library entrypoints, and\nold workflow/environment vocabulary remain unchanged.\n\n## Updated file impact\n\n- src/rebar/llm/reviewers/completion_verifier.md: authoritative affirmative finite banking loop.\n- src/rebar/llm/workflow/completion_banking.py: data-only primary manifest and matching successor\n  system addendum.\n- tests/external/test_completion_banking_behavior_0707.py: pinned repeated RED/GREEN behavioral eval\n  with negative control.\n- tests/unit/workflow/test_completion_banking.py: deterministic authority/manifest/successor\n  contract guards and existing recovery regression suite.\n\n## Testing and verification\n\n- RED/GREEN behavioral gate: `REBAR_RUN_EXTERNAL=1 pytest -q tests/external/test_completion_banking_behavior_0707.py` with the pinned Bedrock Sonnet 4.6 configuration. The test reports three baseline and three repaired trials against the predeclared banking/verdict thresholds and runs the simple-ticket negative control.\n- Deterministic focused suite: `pytest -q tests/unit/workflow/test_completion_banking.py tests/unit/workflow/test_completion_token_recovery.py tests/unit/test_agent_call_runaway_guard_c827.py`.\n- Static gates: `make lint` and `make typecheck` with the current worktree virtualenv first on PATH.\n- Original-scenario replication: run the behavioral harness against `9895-aa2c-8907-4db6` through the read-only `completion.verify_completion(..., source=\"local\", fetch=False)` path and require at least one bank plus a normal PASS/FAIL verdict; do not emit a sidecar or transition the ticket during the probe.\n\n## Phase 2 scope expansion after held-out RED\n\nThree independent prompt-only repairs were rejected by the unchanged live oracle. The pre-fix arm made 17 repository calls before banking all five criteria; moving the finite loop into system authority reduced that to 11, but stronger per-response wording and the model-facing record-tool docstring still produced `[11, 11, 11, 11, 11]` bank points in 3/3 trials. Prompt resolution was inspected at runtime and contained the edited state machine, ruling out a stale prompt. The residual mechanism is parallel/batched tool execution: instruction text cannot mechanically stop multiple repository tools in one model response.\n\nExtend the minimal repair at the existing pydantic-ai toolset wrapper seam. The completion record tool carries private, non-schema metadata naming a three-step repository-evidence cycle. `agent_call.build_agent_kwargs` detects that metadata only for this run and installs a shared wrapper across its toolsets. Per cycle, the wrapper executes at most one read_file/list_directory/search_files call per model response; additional calls in that response receive a steering notice without executing. After three executed evidence responses it continues advertising all tool definitions but steers repository calls to the record transition. One successful record_criterion_verdict call is allowed per response and resets the cycle for the next manifest id. An asyncio lock serializes a parallel batch so the bound is deterministic. All requests without the private metadata remain byte-identical; tool signatures, schemas, return values, public runner/config APIs, completion verdicts, CLI/MCP/library surfaces, and old workflow/environment vocabulary remain unchanged.\n\nAdd deterministic wrapper tests for parallel same-response calls, the three-call boundary, record reset, one-record-per-response, and unflagged-request identity. Keep the held-out live oracle unchanged: current prompt is RED at 0/3 bounded trials, repaired behavior must be GREEN at >=2/3, the simple control must remain PASS, and the exact old no-wrapper defect must return RED. The prompt state machine, data-only manifest, and schema-local finite COMMIT description remain necessary model guidance above the mechanical boundary.\n"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1786268429069759001,"uuid":"717a3dc0-b26c-4093-81e4-d4110a20cbed"}